\subsection{Round-Trip Adaptation Transition}
\label{sec:roundtrip-transition-ablation}

A separate round-trip non-stationary record tests whether the deployed adaptive
schedule follows a sea-state change in both directions rather than only from a
smaller to a larger sea.  The low-sea realization is
$(H_s,T_p)=(\SI{1.5}{m},\SI{5.7}{s})$.  A $C^2$ quintic crossfade to the
high-sea realization $(\SI{4.0}{m},\SI{11.4}{s})$ begins at $t=400$~s and
ends at $t=520$~s.  The high sea is then held until $t=800$~s, when an
identical \SI{120}{s} crossfade returns to the \emph{same phase-randomized
low-sea realization}, ending at $t=920$~s.  The high realization is randomized
independently.  Velocity and acceleration retain the exact first- and
second-derivative blend terms, so the synthetic record remains kinematically
closed through both transitions.

\IfFileExists{ou_rs_roundtrip_transition.svg}{%
  \begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includesvg[width=\columnwidth,inkscapelatex=false]{ou_rs_roundtrip_transition}
    \caption{OU--III Adaptive on one round-trip realization (wave, IMU and
    initialization seeds $11/101/1009$).  Top: the rolling \SI{60}{s}
    significant height the record actually carries, against the
    independent-mixture $H_s$ the quintic weights imply and the linear ramp
    that is \emph{not} what is simulated.  Second: reference and estimated
    vertical displacement.  Third: vertical error.  Bottom: the applied
    $\tau$, $\sigma_{aw}$ and $r_S$ against the two frozen operating points
    calibrated from the low- and high-sea reference records.  The shaded
    intervals are the low-to-high and high-to-low \SI{120}{s} $C^2$ quintic
    crossfades; the dash-dotted line opens the scored \SI{900}{s} window.}
    \label{fig:ou-rs-roundtrip-protocol}
  \end{figure}
}{}

Figure~\ref{fig:ou-rs-roundtrip-protocol} uses the same four-panel diagnostic as
the one-way validation transition (Fig.~\ref{fig:ou_transition}): the sea state,
the wave and its estimate, the vertical error, and the parameters actually
applied by the current scheduler.  It is a transition diagnostic for the
deployed configuration, not a comparison among alternative regularizer laws.

The scored \SI{900}{s} window is partitioned as
\emph{low-start} (300--400~s), \emph{rise} (400--520~s),
\emph{high-recovery} (520--640~s), \emph{high} (640--800~s),
\emph{fall} (800--920~s), \emph{low-recovery} (920--1040~s), and
\emph{low-return} (1040--1200~s).  Rise and fall are therefore reported
separately, as are the run-on intervals after each crossfade.  This prevents
opposite-direction adaptation lags from cancelling in a single transition
score and directly tests whether the schedule that follows increasing sea
energy also releases that tuning when the sea subsides.

The one-way transition in the primary OU-family validation bundle remains a
separate evidence protocol with its own diagnostic
(Fig.~\ref{fig:ou_transition}); the two figures are drawn by the same code and
differ only in the sea-state protocol they replay.
